WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:03.040
I’m often asked
what my opinion

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
about a diet
or disease is.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:11.920
Who cares what my or
anyone else’s opinion is.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:16.000
All we should care about
is what the science says.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.160
What does the best available
balance of evidence

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.080
Published in the peer-
reviewed medical literature

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:21.360
have to say right now?

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.800
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast,

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:27.360
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:31.280
Did you know that research
has consistently shown

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:33.280
that healthcare professionals
are biased

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:36.640
against people classified
as overweight or obese?

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:38.000
In our first story,

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.645
we look at how common
this problem is.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:47.120
Described as the last “acceptable”
form of bias, weight stigma is

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:52.240
the rampant discrimination and
stereotyping of overweight individuals.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.240
Fifty overweight women were asked
to keep a diary of all the times

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:58.400
they felt they were being
stigmatized for their weight.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:03.280
Over a single week, more than
a thousand instances were recorded.

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:06.080
An overweight woman
may expect to be harassed

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:08.160
such as called names or insulted,

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:09.840
run into physical barriers

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.240
(like unable to fit into public seats),

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:15.520
or discriminated against
(such as perceived poorer service

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:19.360
at restaurants or stores)
on average about three times a day.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:24.080
Obese men report three times
less discrimination than women

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:28.800
of the same size; so, maybe it’s
only a daily occurrence for them.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:31.280
They’re not just being paranoid.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:34.880
Studies using professional
actors presenting as job applicants

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:38.000
made up to appear overweight
with the Hollywood magic

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.240
of theatrical prostheses were
significantly more likely to be

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:45.440
discriminated against than when
appearing as their normal weight selves.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.040
This employment bias was
found to be especially prejudiced

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:51.280
against overweight
women compared to men.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:55.120
Attitudes can also be
explored in surveys.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:58.320
In a comparison of 16
stigmatized social groups,

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:03.040
such as homosexuals and homeless
people, only drug addicts and smokers

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:08.320
were regarded with higher levels
of disgust than obese individuals.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:12.080
The researchers did note, however,
there was effectively a tie:

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:18.720
obese people were “rated just
as disgusting as politicians.”

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:21.840
This weight stigma
starts surprisingly young.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:26.240
Children as young as three years
old describe overweight peers as

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:28.880
“mean,” “stupid,” “lazy,” and “ugly.”

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:35.280
Then, there was that famous
study published back in 1961.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.240
Children in summer camps
and schools across a swath

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:40.640
of different social, cultural,
and ethnic backgrounds

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:44.960
in California, Montana, and
New York were asked to rank

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:48.560
the following images as
to who they liked best,

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:52.080
including a child with crutches
with a brace on their leg,

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:56.000
a child in a wheelchair, a child
with one of their hands missing,

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.240
a facially disfigured child, or an obese child.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:03.440
In every population of kids they tested,

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:05.760
there was “remarkable uniformity.”

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:10.400
The obese child always came in dead last

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:14.160
That was ages ago, though.

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:18.560
But in 2003, researchers published
the 40-year follow-up.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:24.080
The study was repeated and...the
title of the study gives it away:

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:29.760
"The Stigmatization of
Obese Children"...getting worse.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.840
The obese child was liked even less!

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:37.200
This parallels trends
throughout society with nearly

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:40.160
a 70 percent jump in
perceived weight discrimination

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:43.600
recorded in national surveys
since the mid-nineties.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:48.160
Attitudes among teachers
may not be helping.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:50.880
More than a quarter of teachers
and other school staff surveyed

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:53.520
felt that becoming obese
is “one of the worst things

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:54.720
that could happen to a person.”

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:59.600
Even parents can be biased,
providing less support for college

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:02.240
for their overweight daughters
compared to thinner siblings.

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:05.440
As two prominent obesity
researchers commented,

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:11.600
“It is a strong prejudice indeed
when parents discriminate

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:13.440
against their own children.”

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:14.400
What about doctors?

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:17.440
One representative national
survey found that more than

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:20.560
half of physicians
viewed obese patients as

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:24.880
“awkward, unattractive, ugly,
and noncompliant.”

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:28.720
About a quarter of nurses agreed
or strongly agreed to the statement:

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:32.640
“Caring for an obese patient
usually repulses me.”

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:37.520
This antagonism can have
serious health consequences

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:39.520
for those who may need it the most.

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:41.600
For example, obese women
are at higher risk

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:45.280
for developing cervical,
endometrial, and ovarian cancers;

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:47.680
yet, they are less likely to be screened.

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:51.360
Morbidly obese patients only
have about half the odds

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:53.280
of getting their
recommended pelvic exams.

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:57.280
Part of this may be avoidance
on the part of the patient,

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:00.240
but some doctors just turn
obese patients away.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:04.960
The Sun Sentinel polled
OB/GYN practices in Florida

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:09.840
and found that as many as one
in seven refused to see heavier women,

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:12.320
for example, setting weight
cut-offs for new patients

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:14.240
starting at 200 pounds.

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:18.320
Even doctors who welcome
obese patients have been found

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:19.680
to give them short shrift.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:22.880
Physicians randomized to
receive a medical chart

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:25.040
of a migraine patient
who was either presented

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:28.800
as average weight, overweight, or
obese said that they would give

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:32.320
the obese patient about
28 percent less of their time.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:35.280
And it’s less quality time.

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:39.360
Recorded doctor’s visits
found physicians tend to build

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:42.320
less emotional rapport
with overweight patients.

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:46.720
Even obesity specialists
profess increasingly

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:49.120
explicit anti-fat attitudes.

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:52.800
Worsening in surveys taken
between 2001 and 2013,

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:55.920
obesity specialists described
fat people as significantly

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:59.360
more “lazy, stupid, and worthless.”

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:04.320
Even in the medical literature,
you’ll find lines like this,

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:06.800
an example from the Annals
of Internal Medicine:

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:11.120
“obesity is an aesthetic crime: it is ugly.”

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:17.040
The good news is they appear
to be able to hide their disdain.

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:22.080
In a study entitled “Obese patients
overestimate physicians’ attitudes

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:26.240
of respect,” despite the
negative attitudes doctors harbored

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:29.600
towards their obese patients,
the same patients expressed

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:31.920
their satisfaction with their providers.

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:35.120
The researchers concluded,
“While physicians may be

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:40.560
successfully playing the part,
the lack of true respect suggests...

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:44.320
the authenticity of the
patient–physician relationship

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:46.477
should be questioned.”

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:49.280
In our final story today we ask,

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:55.086
how might weight stigma
be a vicious cycle?

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:59.920
Although total fasting can
dramatically increase blood levels

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:02.480
of the stress hormone
cortisol, as much as

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:07.600
doubling within five days,
just dieting alone does not.

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:11.440
There is, however, a way
stress and obesity could

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:15.840
turn into a vicious
cycle: weight stigma.

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:20.080
Across thousands of individuals
followed for four years,

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:23.120
those reporting
discriminatory experiences

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:26.160
had more than twice the
odds of becoming obese.

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:28.880
And those who started out obese
had more than three times the odds

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:31.360
of staying that way compared
to those who started out

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:35.520
at the same weight but didn’t
experience discrimination.

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:37.840
Now, this could be from
stress-induced eating

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:40.000
on one side of the
calorie balance equation

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:45.200
or stigma-induced exercise
avoidance on the other.

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:47.920
Obese individuals with more
frequent experiences with

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:52.320
weight stigma report greater
avoidance of exercising in public,

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:54.000
feeling judged and embarrassed.

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:59.120
These “too fat to exercise”
fears may be well-grounded.

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:03.200
Strong anti-fat biases have
been documented in both

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:07.680
fitness professionals and regular
gym-goers, which may translate

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:11.840
into an unwelcoming environment in
fitness centers and health clubs.

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:15.760
Whichever side of the calorie
equation gets tipped,

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:19.200
those who experience weight
stigma can also end up suffering

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:23.040
health consequences
independent of any added weight.

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:27.200
Those reporting more frequent fat
prejudice exhibit higher levels

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:30.880
of depression, higher levels of
inflammation, and higher levels

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:34.400
of oxidative stress, as well
as a shorter lifespan.

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:38.640
Two studies following a
total of nearly 20,000 people

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:41.680
both found about a 50 percent
increase in mortality risk

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:44.400
among those reporting
greater daily discrimination.

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:49.920
Weight discrimination
may shorten life expectancy.

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:52.720
Despite these hazards,
some scholars advocate

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:54.960
for even more fat-shaming.

00:08:55.520 --> 00:08:58.960
The President Emeritus of the
prestigious Hastings Center

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:02.960
infamously advocated for
“a kind of stigmatization lite,”

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:06.400
using social pressures to
compel people to lose weight

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:09.440
without resorting to
outright discrimination.

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:13.520
After all, he argued, what else
has the potential to counter

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:17.280
the persuasive force of the
billions spent in advertising

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:19.520
every year by the
food and beverage industry.

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:21.040
It worked against tobacco.

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:23.680
He recalls his own
battle with addiction:

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:28.160
“The force of being shamed and
beat upon socially was as

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:31.680
persuasive for me to stop smoking
as the threats to my health.”

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:35.520
The public health campaign to
stigmatize cigarettes turned

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:38.160
“what had been considered
simply a bad habit

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:40.960
into reprehensible behavior.”

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:43.280
When such campaigns have been
tried, they have been met

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:45.600
with fierce resistance though.

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:49.520
Georgia’s Strong4Life
campaign featured billboards of

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:52.320
morose-looking obese
children with captions like

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:56.800
“Warning: Chubby kids may
not outlive their parents” or

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:00.560
“It’s hard to be a little
girl when you’re not.”

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:04.960
The campaign sponsors defended
the ads as an attempt

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:07.840
to break through the denial
in a state with some of the

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:10.080
highest recorded
childhood obesity rates.

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:15.120
It’s only defensible, though, if it works.

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:19.040
Yale researchers found
that when normal-weight women

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:21.440
provided with bowls of M&amp;M’s,
jelly beans, and chips

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:25.600
to snack on after watching
clips of stigmatizing material

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:29.360
like clumsy, loud, lazy
stereotypes getting teased

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:32.160
about their weight, they
eat about the same amount

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:36.160
compared to watching neutral
material such as insurance commercials.

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:40.240
But when overweight women
watch the same two sets of videos,

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:43.200
they triple their calorie intakes

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:45.840
after watching the stigmatizing scenes.

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:49.840
The researchers concluded,
“This directly challenges the notion

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:52.720
that pressure to lose weight in
the form of weight stigma will have

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:56.640
a positive, motivating effect
on overweight individuals.”

00:10:57.440 --> 00:10:59.840
In other words, it could
make things worse.

00:11:01.280 --> 00:11:04.720
Being labeled “too fat” in
childhood was associated with a

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:07.360
higher risk of becoming
obese compared to children

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:10.000
weighing the same who
were never told that.

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:13.840
But does that mean we should just
ignore the elephant in the room?

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:17.280
Many doctors apparently think so.

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:20.880
Just as veterinarians have
been found to be reluctant

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:23.360
to tell people their pets are obese,

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:26.320
fewer than a quarter of parents
of overweight children report

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:29.440
having been told by pediatricians
about their child’s weight status.

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:33.200
One might think it would be obvious,

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:35.920
but a Gallup Survey found
that parents appear to be

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:39.280
“notoriously poor judges
of their children’s weight.”

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:43.200
Similarly, the percentage of
adults who describe themselves

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:45.840
as overweight has remained
essentially unchanged

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:50.000
over the past few decades,
despite skyrocketing obesity.

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:54.640
All this, Gallup concluded,
helps “paint a picture of

00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:59.440
mass delusion in the United
States about its rising weight.”

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:04.480
I think patients have
the right to be informed.

00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:06.880
Those told by their doctor
that they are overweight

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:09.360
have about four times the
odds of attempting weight loss

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:12.240
and about twice the
odds of succeeding.

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:16.320
Just as smoking physicians
are less likely to challenge

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:19.920
their smoking patients, overweight
physicians are less likely

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:21.600
to bring up the subject of weight loss

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:24.640
or even document obesity in their charts.

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:28.960
Ironically, overweight
patients trust diet advice

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:32.480
from overweight doctors more
than docs who are normal weight.

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:35.760
Unfortunately, primary
care physicians appear

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:38.320
to have little to offer
in terms of specifics.

00:12:38.320 --> 00:12:40.560
Fewer than half who were
surveyed said they provide

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:42.240
specific advice to their patients.

00:12:42.880 --> 00:12:45.360
Just telling patients
to “watch what they eat”

00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:48.480
is unlikely to be particularly helpful,

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:51.840
but many primary care physicians
may not even get that far.

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:55.440
Most physicians said they
would spend more time working

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:57.920
with patients on weight
management if only their time

00:12:57.920 --> 00:13:00.480
was “reimbursed appropriately.”

00:13:02.080 --> 00:13:06.800
Maybe we could offer a bonus to
refrain from blaming the victim.

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:10.480
One pair of commentators wrote
in response to the pro-stigma camp,

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:15.520
“If shaming reduced obesity,
there would be no fat people.”

00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:20.240
I want to end this weight
stigma video series with the

00:13:20.240 --> 00:13:24.640
jaw-dropping findings of a study
that I think best illustrates

00:13:24.640 --> 00:13:27.920
how hard it is to
live inside a fat body.

00:13:28.720 --> 00:13:33.200
If this doesn’t foster sympathy
among my medical colleagues,

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:35.040
I don’t know what will.

00:13:36.160 --> 00:13:39.840
Researchers talked to men and
women who had lost and kept off

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:43.200
more than 100 pounds to
tap into their unique insight,

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:45.680
having personally experienced
what it was like to be

00:13:45.680 --> 00:13:49.440
morbidly obese and then,
on average, 126 pounds lighter.

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:53.280
Forty-seven such
individuals were interviewed.

00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:56.880
They were asked to think back
to when they were heavier

00:13:56.880 --> 00:14:00.600
and make a choice: “If someone
offered you a couple of

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:03.920
million dollars if you
stayed morbidly obese forever,

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:05.680
would you have chosen the money?

00:14:05.680 --> 00:14:08.720
Or would you have chosen to be
normal weight no matter what?”

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:14.480
Option #1 was, “I would have chosen
no money and being normal weight.

00:14:14.480 --> 00:14:17.360
It would have taken me
about a second to decide.”

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:22.960
Option #2 was, “I probably would
have chosen being normal weight.

00:14:22.960 --> 00:14:25.360
But the possibility of
having that much money

00:14:25.360 --> 00:14:27.280
would make me think about the choice.”

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:30.880
Option #3 was,
“I wanted to be normal weight,

00:14:30.880 --> 00:14:32.720
but I really could use the money.

00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:34.560
If I would be a multimillionaire,

00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:36.880
I think I could live
with being morbidly obese.”

00:14:37.600 --> 00:14:40.880
One of the 47 had to think about it,

00:14:41.440 --> 00:14:43.840
but the other 46 jumped at Option 1.

00:14:45.040 --> 00:14:47.360
No one chose Option 3.

00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:50.800
They all said they would give
up being a multimillionaire

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:51.920
to be normal weight.

00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:57.680
If that shocked you,
buckle your seatbelts.

00:14:58.960 --> 00:15:03.520
They were then asked about being
obese compared to other disabilities.

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:06.240
Normally, when you ask people
to choose between living with

00:15:06.240 --> 00:15:08.960
their own disability or
switching to a different one,

00:15:08.960 --> 00:15:12.560
there is a strong proclivity
to stay with their own.

00:15:12.560 --> 00:15:14.000
For example, even though most people

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:16.000
would rather be deaf than blind,

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:19.760
blind people prefer to remain
blind by a large margin,

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:22.160
rather than having
sight without sound.

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:25.440
They already know how to cope
with their own disability,

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:27.520
and so there’s safety in familiarity.

00:15:28.160 --> 00:15:30.960
However, the exact opposite happened

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:33.840
when the formerly obese were asked.

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:38.320
Every single one of the 47
said they’d rather be deaf

00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:40.880
for the rest of their lives than obese.

00:15:42.400 --> 00:15:46.560
Every single one said they’d
rather have difficulty reading,

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:51.360
be diabetic, have very bad acne,
or heart disease than be obese.

00:15:52.400 --> 00:15:56.400
More than 90 percent said
they’d rather have a leg amputated,

00:15:56.400 --> 00:15:59.840
and similarly, about 9 out of 10 said

00:15:59.840 --> 00:16:05.520
they’d rather be blind
their whole lives than obese.

00:16:07.600 --> 00:16:11.280
Obesity appears to be the
only handicap where nearly everyone

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:13.680
wants to switch, no
matter what the cost.

00:16:14.800 --> 00:16:16.480
To quote one study subject,

00:16:17.360 --> 00:16:20.080
”When you’re blind,
people want to help you.

00:16:20.960 --> 00:16:24.612
No one wants to help when you’re fat.”

